1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.102
Before you send your first prompt,

2
00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:04.971
let's talk a little bit about setting up your workspace.

3
00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:08.575
If you're already comfortable with setting up a coding agent

4
00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:10.577
like Claude Code in an IDE

5
00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:14.481
such as WebStorm, feel free to skip this optional video.

6
00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:17.951
Spec-Driven Development is a best practice

7
00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:21.621
that isn't tied to any specific IDE or coding agent.

8
00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:24.958
So, you can choose the setup you're already using

9
00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:27.293
or the same one you'll see in this course.

10
00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:30.530
VS Code with the Codex CLI,

11
00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.732
Zed editor with a local model,

12
00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:35.535
all great for spec-driven development.

13
00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:38.538
Since we are planning to develop a web application,

14
00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:41.541
this course will use the WebStorm IDE

15
00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:44.544
with Claude Code as a coding agent.

16
00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:47.714
Instructions on downloading and installing both

17
00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:50.517
have been included in the previous reading item.

18
00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:52.852
Let's open up WebStorm and start

19
00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:55.422
a brand new project named Agent Clinic.

20
00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:58.124
This will be a TypeScript project

21
00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:01.528
with a Git repository to keep close

22
00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:04.130
track of versioning your code and specs.

23
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:10.937
Although many IDEs, including WebStorm, offer a chat panel,

24
00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:13.239
we know there's a lot of diversity

25
00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.442
in how you interact with your coding agents.

26
00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:18.545
In spec-driven development with agents,

27
00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:22.048
it's important to keep close track of versioning your code.

28
00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:26.252
Let's say we want to create an initial commit using the agent.

29
00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:29.289
Every time it needs to execute a command,

30
00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:31.591
it will ask you for the confirmation

31
00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:35.195
unless you start Claude Code in an unsafe mode.

32
00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:39.165
Pay close attention to what Claude Code asks you to do.

33
00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:42.669
The ultimate responsibility for the code is yours.

34
00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:47.340
Throughout the coming lessons, you'll see several tricks and best practices

35
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.342
for spec-driven development with Git.

36
00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:52.145
Great, we've got our setup ready and

37
00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:54.347
you've got yours ready. Let's get started.
